        <section id="introduction">
            <h2>Introduction</h2>
            <p>The internet is one of the most important inventions of the modern world. It allows people to communicate, learn, work, shop and share information across long distances. We use it every day through phones, computers and other connected devices. However, many people do not know what happens when they open a website or send a message. The internet is a global network of connected computers and servers that exchange information using agreed communication rules. Understanding its basic working helps us use technology more effectively and responsibly.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>How Does the Internet Work?</h2>
            <p>When you enter a website address in your browser, your device contacts a Domain Name System (DNS) server to find the corresponding IP address. Your browser then sends a request to the website's server. The server processes the request and sends the required information back to your device. Your browser uses HTML and other web technologies to display the page. Information is divided into small units called packets, which travel through networks and are reassembled at the destination.</p>
            <p>Routers help direct data between networks, while internet service providers connect homes and organisations to the wider internet. Different routes may be used to deliver packets, depending on network conditions.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="important-parts">
            <h2>Important Parts of the Internet</h2>
            <ol>
                <li><strong>Client:</strong> A device or application that requests information.</li>
                <li><strong>Server:</strong> A computer that stores or provides websites and services.</li>
                <li><strong>Router:</strong> A device that forwards data between networks.</li>
                <li><strong>DNS:</strong> A system that translates domain names into IP addresses.</li>
            </ol>
            <h3>Common Uses</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Online education and research</li>
                <li>Communication through email and messaging</li>
                <li>Entertainment, music and videos</li>
                <li>Online shopping and digital payments</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

